WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 29: Rhyolite: The Ghost Metropolis of the Bullfrog Hills

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:16.800
[Rebecca] The history of the American West is littered with ghost towns, but none was more grand, sophisticated, or short-lived than the vanished city of Ryolite, Nevada.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:24.560
[Rebecca] In 1904, prospectors discovered a vein of gold so rich in green quartz that it was nicknamed the Bullfrog Strike.

5
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:42.160
[Rebecca] Within three years, an empty alkaline desert near the edge of Death Valley exploded into a glittering metropolis of 10,000 residents, complete with concrete bank buildings, swimming pools, electric streetlights, and an ornate railway station that rivaled European terminals.

6
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:56.720
[Rebecca] Yet in an astonishing blink of history, the boom crashed, the money vanished, and in less than five years, the entire city dissolved back into the desert dust, leaving behind ghostly concrete ruins that still haunt the desert today.

7
00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.440
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

8
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:23.520
[George] While hunting for stray pack burrows on a desolate volcanic ridge, Harris chipped away at a promising boulder of green-veined quartz rock.

9
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.520
[George] Peering closely through his jeweler's loop, Harris was stunned.

10
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:35.200
[George] The rock was crisscrossed with rich stringers of pure native gold glowing like yellow moss against the sea-green stone.

11
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.840
[George] Harris exclaimed, This rock looks just like the back of a bullfrog.

12
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:50.800
[George] The strike was named the Bullfrog Mine, and within days of registering the claim in Goldfield, news of the discovery triggered a wild, stampeding gold rush across the Great Basin.

13
00:01:50.800 --> 00:02:05.160
[George] Shorty Harris, a legendary frontier character who stood barely five feet tall and spent his entire life chasing phantom strikes across the Mojave, celebrated the discovery with an infamous weeklong drinking spree in Goldfield.

14
00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:19.160
[George] In a haze of saloon whiskey, Harris sold his half interest in the multimillion dollar bullfrog claim for a mere $1,000 and a pack mule, a blunder he spent the rest of his life chuckling about around desert campfires.

15
00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:27.800
[George] Meanwhile, thousands of miners, gamblers, speculators and merchants converged on the barren basin in the shadow of Montgomery Mountain.

16
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:39.720
[George] Where only rattlesnakes and creosote bushes had existed months before, a sprawling tent city erupted that took the name of Rhyolite, after the volcanic silica rock that encased the golden veins.

17
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:53.560
[George] Unlike typical frontier mining camps that were hastily thrown together out of flimsy pine shacks and canvas tents, Ryolite's ambitious promoters envisioned a permanent, grand industrial metropolis that would endure for centuries.

18
00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:59.160
[George] Backed by millions of dollars in eastern venture capital from Pittsburgh steel magnate Charles M.

19
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.040
[George] Schwab, Montana Copper Baron William A.

20
00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:09.400
[George] Clark, and wealthy Philadelphia financiers, Ryolite was constructed with opulent, state-of-the-art infrastructure.

21
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:23.480
[George] The town quickly boasted poured concrete curbs and sidewalks, a telephone exchange connecting directly to Lowe's Angels, an electrical grid powered by a hydroelectric plant high in the Sierra Nevada at Bishop, California.

22
00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:28.120
[George] And bright electric arc streetlights illuminating 53 city blocks.

23
00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:33.000
[George] By 1907, the population had swelled to over 10,000 people.

24
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:37.560
[George] The desert skyline featured impressive architectural monuments, the John S.

25
00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.160
[George] Cook and Company Bank.

26
00:03:39.160 --> 00:04:04.640
[George] An imposing three-story structure built of reinforced concrete and ashler stone with imported italian marble counters and hand-carved mahogany woodwork, the four-story Overberry building, which housed high-end corporate brokerage offices and mining syndicates, an opera house, two public schools, three weekly newspapers, and a 40-bed hospital equipped with the latest diagnostic X-ray technology.

27
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:17.120
[George] Residents enjoyed fresh Pacific oysters shipped daily on ice, vintage French champagne, and a public swimming pool that cost $20,000 to construct in the middle of one of the driest deserts on Earth.

28
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:26.160
[George] Amidst the grand concrete banks and opera houses, one eccentric building captured the world's imagination, the Tom Kelly Bottle House.

29
00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:41.440
[George] In 1905, an Australian-born prospector and stonemason named Tom Kelly decided that lumber in the treeless desert was far too expensive, costing up to $100 per thousand board feet after being hauled hundreds of miles by freight wagon.

30
00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:47.280
[George] However, Ryolite's fifty saloons produced an endless, free supply of empty glassware.

31
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:56.240
[George] Kelly spent months scouring the town's alleys and saloon rubbish heaps, collecting over 50,000 empty beer, whiskey and medicine bottles.

32
00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:07.840
[George] Laying the bottles on their sides in thick adobe mortar with their bases pointing outward, Kelly built a complete three-bedroom home with decorative gables, a porch, and interior partitions.

33
00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:16.560
[George] Sunlight streaming through the green, amber, and clear glass bottles illuminated the interior in kaleidoscopic, jewel-toned colors.

34
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:26.080
[George] The bottle house became an instant tourist sensation, featured in magazines across Europe and America as a triumph of frontier resourcefulness and humor.

35
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.080
[George] It stands to this day as one of the oldest and best preserved bottle houses in the world.

36
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:35.880
[George] The crowning achievement of Ryolite's dizzying ascendancy was its railroad connection.

37
00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:45.640
[George] For a boomtown to achieve lasting commercial dominance, it required direct rail access to transport heavy stamp mill machinery and haul ore to smelters.

38
00:05:45.640 --> 00:06:05.080
[George] The promise of the Bullfrog District was so intoxicating that three separate, competing railroad empires raced across the desert to build lines into Ryolite, the Las Vegas and Tonapa Railroad, the Bullfrog Goldfield Railroad, and Copper Baron Borax Smith's Tonapa and Tidewater Railroad.

39
00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:10.040
[George] By 1907, three passenger trains arrived daily in Ryolite.

40
00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:21.640
[George] To celebrate its triumph, the Las Vegas and Tonapa Railroad constructed a magnificent two-story Mission Revival style passenger depot costing $130,000.

41
00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:34.120
[George] Built of polished white stone and arched terracotta tile, the station featured a lavish dining room, a baggage room, and luxury passenger waiting lounges that resembled a grand Mediterranean villa.

42
00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:44.680
[George] Dignitaries and millionaires stepped off luxury pullman parlor cars directly into the heart of the desert, convinced that Ryolite was destined to become the premier metropolis of Nevada.

43
00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:51.560
[George] Yet beneath the glittering concrete facades and marble bank counters lay a fatal, hidden geological reality.

44
00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:56.680
[George] The gold simply was not there in the quantities that promoters and mine brokers had claimed.

45
00:06:56.680 --> 00:07:10.840
[George] The surface ore of the bullfrog strike had been spectacularly rich, but as mining shafts plunged deeper into Montgomery Mountain, the gold veins pinched out, broken, faulted, and scattered by ancient tectonic fractures.

46
00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:23.360
[George] At 300 feet underground, the rich sea green quartz turned abruptly into barren, low-grade rhyolite rock that cost significantly more to drill, blast, and haul than it was worth in bullion.

47
00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.160
[George] The fatal blow fell in 1907.

48
00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:38.480
[George] First, the catastrophic San Francisco earthquake of 1906 redirected millions in venture capital to rebuilding the ruined coastal metropolis, depriving Nevada mines of liquidity.

49
00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:48.960
[George] Then, in the autumn of 1907, a nationwide financial catastrophe known as the Panic of 1907 seized Wall Street, when copper speculator F.

50
00:07:48.960 --> 00:08:00.960
[George] Augustus Hines' attempt to corner the stock of United Copper collapsed, a wave of bank runs brought down the Knickerbocker Trust Company in New York, freezing commercial credit across the nation overnight.

51
00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:09.840
[George] Eastern brokerage firms collapsed, and Charles Schwab and other industrial capitalists abruptly shut off all funding for Ryolite's mines.

52
00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:19.520
[George] Without continuous outside investment to subsidize operations, the mines began closing one by one, laying off hundreds of miners and mill hands.

53
00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:30.320
[George] The Montgomery-Shoshone mine, the district's crown jewel, struggled along under mounting debts, but in 1911, its heavy stamp mill fell silent forever.

54
00:08:30.320 --> 00:08:36.720
[George] When the mines shut their iron gates, the collapse of Ryolite was as breathtakingly swift as its rise.

55
00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.760
[George] The boomtown evaporated almost overnight.

56
00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:43.200
[George] In 1908, the population was 8,000.

57
00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.040
[George] By 1910, it had plummeted to fewer than 600.

58
00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:55.680
[George] By 1912, the banks shut their doors, power lines were de-energized, and the grand streetlights went dark for the last time.

59
00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:06.440
[George] Families abandoned modern homes with dining room tables still set and pianos left in living rooms, unable to afford the freight charges to ship heavy furniture out of the desert.

60
00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:10.920
[George] Merchants locked their plate glass store doors and threw the keys into the dust.

61
00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:23.960
[George] Then came the scavengers, because lumber and building supplies were at an extreme premium in newly booming settlements like Las Vegas and Beattie, dismantling crews moved into Ryolite with crowbars and flatcars.

62
00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:32.200
[George] Entire wooden houses, hotels and saloons were disassembled board by board, loaded onto rail cars and hauled away.

63
00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:44.280
[George] In 1916, the Las Vegas and Tanapa Railroad discontinued service and tore up forty miles of steel track to sell for scrap metal to support Allied munitions in World War One.

64
00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:53.240
[George] By 1920, Ryolite had a population of exactly 14 residents, and its final citizen, an eccentric French caretaker named J.

65
00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:53.480
[George] D.

66
00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:58.360
[George] Bush, who lived in the abandoned train depot, passed away in 1924.

67
00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:03.080
[George] With human life extinguished, the desert stepped in to reclaim its kingdom.

68
00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:09.880
[George] Flash floods washed through the paved streets, and howling sandstorms blasted the plaster from concrete walls.

69
00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:16.120
[George] Today, Ryolite is world famous as one of the most haunting and photogenic ghost towns on the planet.

70
00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:29.880
[George] Rising majestically from the creosote bushes against the stark desert ridges stand the hollow, roofless skeletons of the three-story cookbank and the Overbury building, their gaping window arches framing the deep blue Nevada sky.

71
00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:43.480
[George] In 1984, Belgian artists created the Goldwell Open Air Museum on the town's edge, erecting life-sized ghostly white plaster figures recreating Leonardo da Vinci's Last Supper in the silent desert sands.

72
00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:56.480
[George] Ryolite remains an unforgettable monument to the intoxicating hubris of the American frontier, a dazzling city of glass, concrete, and electric dreams that bloomed and died in the blink of an eye.

73
00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:09.680
[Rebecca] It whispers that even the grandest cities of concrete and marble are only as permanent as the dreams that build them.

74
00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:12.720
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

75
00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.000
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

76
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:18.320
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we continue our journey across the American saga.

77
00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.720
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

